The '''legislative districts of Pasay''' are the representations of the [[Cities of the Philippines#Independent cities|highly urbanized city]] of [[Pasay]] in the [[Congress of the Philippines]]. The city is currently represented in the [[House of Representatives of the Philippines|lower house]] of the Congress through its [[#Lone District|lone congressional district]].

== History ==
{{further|Legislative districts of Rizal|Legislative districts of Manila}}
Pasay was initially represented as part of the [[Manila's at-large congressional district|at-large district]] of the [[Manila (province)|province of Manila]] in the [[Malolos Congress]] from 1898 to 1899. The then-town was later incorporated to the province of [[Rizal (province)|Rizal]], established in 1901, and was represented as part of the [[Rizal's 1st congressional district|first district]] of Rizal from 1907 to 1941 and from 1945 to 1972. It was incorporated to the [[City of Greater Manila]] during [[World War II]] and was represented as part of the at-large district of Manila from 1943 to 1944. <!-- Areas south of the Maricaban Creek and Estero de Tripa de Gallina (including [[Nichols Field]]) that are currently under the city's jurisdiction, meanwhile, apparently remained part of the first district of Rizal during the war-time legislature.<ref>{{cite map |author = |title = Map of the City of Manila |trans-title = |map = |map-url = |date = |year = 1942 |url =https://assets.isu.pub/document-structure/210327135441-504a42fce9e9c0dafe948e678988e3b0/v1/68eac8a829832842ad677a95008f9037.jpg |scale = 1:40,000 |series = |publisher = Division of Drafting and Surveys, Office of the City Engineer and Architect, City of Manila |cartography = |page = |pages = |section = |sections = |inset = |edition = |location = |language = |format = |isbn = |id = |ref = |access-date =August 30, 2022 |archive-url = |archive-date =}}</ref> --> Pasay was separated from Rizal on November 7, 1975 by virtue of Presidential Decree No. 824,<ref name=pd824>{{cite PH act|url=http://www.lawphil.net/statutes/presdecs/pd1975/pd_824_1975.html |chamber=PD|number=824|date=November 7, 1975|access-date=October 10, 2017|title=Creating the Metropolitan Manila and the Metropolitan Manila Commission and for Other Purposes}}</ref> and was represented in the [[Interim Batasang Pambansa]] along with other [[Metro Manila|Metropolitan Manila]] municipalities and cities as part of [[Interim Batasang Pambansa#Regional Representatives|Region IV]] from 1978 to 1984.

Pasay first gained separate representation in [[1984 Philippine parliamentary election|1984]], when it elected one assemblyman at-large to the [[Regular Batasang Pambansa]] from 1984 to 1986. The city continued to constitute a separate [[House of Representatives of the Philippines#District representation|congressional district]] under the new Constitution<ref>{{cite web|title=1987 Constitution of the Philippines - Apportionment Ordinance|url=http://www.officialgazette.gov.ph/constitutions/the-1987-constitution-of-the-republic-of-the-philippines/the-1987-constitution-of-the-republic-of-the-philippines-ordinance/|author=1986 Constitutional Commission|date=February 2, 1987|access-date=October 9, 2017}}</ref> proclaimed on February 11, 1987; it elected its member to the restored House of Representatives starting [[1987 Philippine House of Representatives elections|that same year]].

